────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  RECOMMENDATION FOR FINE-TUNING
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Based on 22 metrics compared:
  FlanT5-XL wins on more metrics overall.

  Key considerations:
    - FlanT5 is instruction-tuned → understands prompts better
    - FlanT5 has severe repetition/hallucination in ingredients (7k+ hallucinated)
    - OPT has many unparseable outputs (not instruction-tuned)
    - FlanT5 is better at captioning and calorie estimation
    - OPT is better at ingredient precision and macro estimation
    - Both are near-random on VQA (~57% accuracy)

  Fine-tuning can fix:
    - FlanT5's repetition loops (LoRA teaches it when to stop)
    - Numeric accuracy for both (LoRA on Nutrition5K data)
    - VQA accuracy (LoRA on task-specific QA pairs)

  Fine-tuning CANNOT easily fix:
    - OPT's fundamental lack of instruction understanding
    - OPT's high unparseable rate (needs prompt engineering, not just LoRA)

  Suggested model for LoRA fine-tuning: FlanT5-XL
    Rationale: Its instruction-following ability gives LoRA a better
    starting point. The repetition problem is exactly the kind of issue
    LoRA fine-tuning on Nutrition5K can fix. OPT's issues are more
    fundamental — it doesn't understand what we're asking it to do.

